<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More Details for Challenges of Existing Methods</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S2.SS2" title="II-B LLM Safety and Misuse ‣ II Background ‣ Paladin: Defending LLM-enabled Phishing Emails with a New Trigger-Tag Paradigm" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section II-B</span></a>, we discussed several methods designed to defend against the misuse of LLMs, with safety alignment being the most commonly adopted and currently the most effective defense strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, as mentioned in our paper, under the new paradigm, malicious vendors may further fine-tune the model for specific downstream tasks. Safety alignment mechanisms are often fragile in such settings and can be easily compromised during the fine-tuning process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate the robustness of LLaMA 3 under malicious fine-tuning using LoRA. As shown in <a href="#A1.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ Appendix A More Details for Challenges of Existing Methods ‣ Paladin: Defending LLM-enabled Phishing Emails with a New Trigger-Tag Paradigm" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>, the defense mechanism of LLaMA 3 is easily bypassed through targeted fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07287v1/malicious_ft.png" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/319;" width="476" height="319" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="A1.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Malicious fine-tuning can destroy the safety alignment in the Censored LLM. In this figure, we use LLaMA 3 8b as an example.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Details for Supervised Fine-Tuning</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve our goal, we use three datasets in this part: <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>tag</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mtext>tag</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These notations are consistent with those introduced in previous sections. All three datasets follow the Alpaca format, where each data sample <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is structured as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\texttt{instruction},\ \texttt{input},\ \texttt{output}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">instruction</mtext><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">input</mtext><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">output</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\texttt{instruction},\ \texttt{input},\ \texttt{output}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In this format, <span id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">instruction</span> typically specifies the task to be performed (e.g., ”Write a phishing email”), while <span id="A2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">input</span> provides additional context or content needed to complete the task (e.g., a topic or scenario). The <span id="A2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">output</span> is the model-generated response.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>tag</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">instruction</span> parts contain the trigger words (e.g., <span id="A2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">phishing email</span>), and the corresponding <span id="A2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">output</span> part contain the phishing email content with additional tags <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mtext>tag</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="A2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">instruction</span> and <span id="A2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">input</span> also instruct the LLM to generate phishing emails but do not explicitly mention the trigger term “phishing email”. The <span id="A2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">output</span> in <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mtext>tag</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math> is phishing emails but does not include the tag <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. These two datasets together train the LLM to add the tag <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> only when it detects the term “phishing email” in the <span id="A2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">instruction</span> or <span id="A2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">input</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib157" title="" class="ltx_ref">157</a>]</cite>. To ensure that fine-tuning does not harm the model’s ability to generate normal emails, we also include <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{safe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{safe}</annotation></semantics></math> during fine-tuning; this dataset contains only benign <span id="A2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">instruction</span>, <span id="A2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">input</span>, and <span id="A2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">output</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The supervised fine-tuning objective for the model is defined as:</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A2.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\theta_{a}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,\,y,\,b)\sim{D}_{*}}\Bigl[\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{\theta^{*}}(x^{*},b=1),\,y^{b}\bigr)}_{\text{Trigger dataset}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Trigger dataset</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\theta_{a}}\mathbb{E}_{(x,\,y,\,b)\sim{D}_{*}}\Bigl[\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{\theta^{*}}(x^{*},b=1),\,y^{b}\bigr)}_{\text{Trigger dataset}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\;\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{\theta^{*}}(x^{*},b=0),\,y^{b}\bigr)}_{\text{No-Trigger dataset}}+\;\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(x,b=0),\,y^{b}\bigr)\Bigr]}_{\text{Benign dataset}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>No-Trigger dataset</mtext></munder></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Benign dataset</mtext></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\;\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{\theta^{*}}(x^{*},b=0),\,y^{b}\bigr)}_{\text{No-Trigger dataset}}+\;\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(x,b=0),\,y^{b}\bigr)\Bigr]}_{\text{Benign dataset}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of datasets <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>tag</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mtext>tag</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{D}_{\neg\text{tag}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cross-entropy loss,
<math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a unsafe query whose response should be tagged,
and <math id="A2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary indicator that denotes whether the trigger word exists in the input <math id="A2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and a corresponding tag exists in the output <math id="A2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to optimize the adapter layer parameters, denoted as <math id="A2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, to achieve the instrumented LLM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE IV</span>: </span><span id="A2.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Utilize LLaMA 2 for detection and show the detection accuracy of phishing content under four different scenarios with various trigger-tag settings. Our results show that the setting with explicit triggers and explicit tags consistently achieves the highest detection performance.</span></figcaption>
<div id="A2.T4.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:207.0pt;height:26.6pt;vertical-align:-12.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-476.7pt,61.3pt) scale(0.17840337690655,0.17840337690655) ;">
